course: world
lesson: 104
title: Timetables: buses, trains, TV and the school day
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A timetable is a promise written as a list of times: when the bus comes, when the train leaves and arrives, when the cartoon starts, when lunch ends. It lets thousands of people meet the same bus without phoning each other.
explain: Callum's bus comes at 8:10, 8:25 and 8:40. He reaches the stop at 8:12: "Missed it by two flippin' minutes!" Now he waits for the 8:25, which is 13 more minutes. Always know the next time too, not just the one you want.
explain: To find how long something lasts, count from the start time to the end time. If you cross an o'clock, hop to the o'clock first, then add the rest: from 16:50 to 17:30 is 10 minutes to 17:00 plus 30 more, 40 minutes.
explain: Read the small print. "Mon–Fri" means Monday to Friday only; "last entry 5:15" is earlier than closing time; "+1" means the train arrives the next day; and the paper timetable is only the plan, while a live app shows where the bus really is.
fact: A timetable is a list of times when things such as buses, trains, lessons or TV programmes happen.
fact: To find how long something lasts, take the start time from the end time, or count on from the start to the end.
fact: When counting across an o'clock, go up to the o'clock first and then add the rest of the minutes.
fact: If you miss a bus, you wait for the next one, so it helps to know the time after it too.
fact: "Buses every 12 minutes" from 7:00 means 7:12, 7:24, 7:36 and so on; not every round time like 7:30 is on the list.
fact: "Mon–Fri" means Monday to Friday, the 5 weekdays; Saturday and Sunday are not included.
fact: Many places have a "last entry" time before closing time, and after last entry you cannot go in even though they are still open.
fact: On long-distance timetables "+1" after a time means the arrival is on the next day.
fact: A train leaving at 23:40 and arriving at 06:10 the next day takes 6 hours 30 minutes: 20 minutes to midnight and 6 hours 10 minutes after it.
fact: Traffic, weather and roadworks make buses late, so a live app saying "Due 3:47" for a 3:40 bus means it is about 7 minutes late.
fact: TV guides and train timetables often use the 24-hour clock, where 17:30 is 5:30 pm.
fact: In the USA a school break is often called recess.
fact: Words people use: "flippin'" is a mild swear word (UK), "proper" means very (UK), "bruv" means brother or mate (London), "fixin' to" means about to (southern USA) and "can or not?" means "is it possible?" (Singapore).
exercise: Callum's buses come at 8:10, 8:25 and 8:40. He arrives at 8:12. How long does he wait for the next bus? | 13 minutes | 2 minutes | 15 minutes | 28 minutes
exercise: By how many minutes did Callum miss the 8:10 bus? | 2 minutes | 12 minutes | 10 minutes | 13 minutes
exercise: Lunch is 12:15 to 1:15. How long is lunch? | 1 hour | 1 hour 15 minutes | 45 minutes | 2 hours
exercise: Break is 10:30 to 10:45. How long is break? | 15 minutes | 30 minutes | 45 minutes | 10 minutes
exercise: Lessons start at 8:50 and home time is 3:15. How long is the school day? | 6 hours 25 minutes | 5 hours 35 minutes | 7 hours 25 minutes | 6 hours 35 minutes
exercise: Tom's train leaves Leeds at 09:05 and arrives in York at 09:28. How long is the journey? | 23 minutes | About an hour | 33 minutes | 28 minutes
exercise: Maya's cartoon is at 17:30. It is 16:50. How long must she wait? | 40 minutes | 1 hour 20 minutes | 20 minutes | 60 minutes
exercise: A timetable says "Mon–Fri". On which day will Lucas find no bus? | Saturday | Monday | Wednesday | Friday
exercise: Buses come every 12 minutes from 7:00. Which of these is a bus time? | 7:36 | 7:30 | 7:20 | 7:40
exercise: The pool's last entry is 5:15 and it closes at 6:00. Josh arrives at 5:20. Can he swim? | No, he is 5 minutes after last entry | Yes, there are 40 minutes before closing | Yes, last entry is the same as closing | No, the pool closed at 5:00
exercise: When Josh arrives at 5:20, how long is it until the pool closes at 6:00? | 40 minutes | 20 minutes | 45 minutes | 5 minutes
exercise: Arjun's train leaves at 23:40 and arrives at 06:10 "+1". What does "+1" mean? | It arrives the next day | It costs one pound more | It has one extra carriage | It is one hour late
exercise: How long is Arjun's journey from 23:40 to 06:10 the next day? | 6 hours 30 minutes | 17 hours 30 minutes | 7 hours 30 minutes | 5 hours 30 minutes
exercise: The bus is meant to come at 3:40, but the app says "Due 3:47". How late is it? | 7 minutes | 47 minutes | 3 minutes | 13 minutes
exercise: Aditi's dance class starts at 4:30 and lasts 45 minutes. When should Mum pick her up? | 5:15 | 5:45 | 4:75 | 5:00
exercise: What is the difference between a timetable and a live app? | The timetable is the plan; the app shows where the bus really is | The timetable is always more exact than the app | The app shows only trains, not buses | There is no difference between them
test: Tom's mate reckons Leeds 09:05 to York 09:28 takes "about an hour". Is he right? | No, it is only 23 minutes | Yes, about an hour is right | No, it is 1 hour 23 minutes | No, it takes 9 hours
test: Lucas turns up on Saturday at a "Mon–Fri" stop. "Proper useless," he grumbles. What should he have done? | Read which days the timetable covers | Waited longer, as Saturday buses come late | Gone to the stop on Sunday instead | Nothing; buses always run every day
test: Jade, Singapore style: "Bus every 12 minutes from seven, want to catch at 7:30, can or not?" What's the answer? | Not exactly; buses come at 7:24 and 7:36 | Yes, there is a bus at exactly 7:30 | No, the next bus after 7:00 is at 8:00 | Yes, buses come at 7:15 and 7:30
test: Fern's mate goes, "Buses always lie, bruv," when the app says 3:47 for a 3:40 bus. What's the fairest answer? | The timetable is a plan, and traffic made the bus 7 minutes late | Buses always come 7 minutes before the timetable | The app is lying and the bus left at 3:40 | Timetables are never right, so ignore them
test: The lifeguard says no to Josh at 5:20, even though the pool closes at 6. Why? | Last entry was 5:15, before closing time | The lifeguard was just being mean | The pool had already closed at 5:00 | Swimming always stops 40 minutes early
test: Maya's grandpa, a proper boomer: "Back in my day we had no TV guide." Maya's cartoon is at 17:30 and it's 16:50. How long till it starts? | 40 minutes | 50 minutes | 1 hour 40 minutes | 20 minutes
test: A Texan cousin says, "We're fixin' to leave for the 4:30 dance class; it's 45 minutes." When does the class end? | 5:15 | 5:45 | 5:30 | 4:75
test: Arjun says, "The train's 23:40 to 06:10, so it's 17 hours." What went wrong? | He subtracted the wrong way; across midnight it is 6 hours 30 minutes | He is right, it is 17 hours long | It is really 6 hours exactly | It is really 7 hours 10 minutes
test: What would happen if Callum knew only the 8:10 bus time and missed it? | He would not know how long to wait for the next one | The 8:10 bus would come back for him | No more buses would come that day | The timetable would change for him
test: Erin, bored: "Lunch is 12:15 to 1:15, so is lunch, like, 15 minutes?" | No, it is a whole hour | Yes, it is 15 minutes | No, it is 1 hour 30 minutes | No, it is 45 minutes
test: A cartoon shows a train timetable with "+1" next to a time, and the hero thinks it means "one minute late". Put it right. | It means the train arrives the next day | It means the train is one hour late | It means one extra stop on the way | It means the ticket costs one more
exam: It is given that a train departs at 09:05 and arrives at 09:28. Determine the duration of the journey. | Twenty-three minutes | Thirty-three minutes | Twenty-eight minutes | One hour
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the notation "+1" appended to an arrival time upon a long-distance timetable. | Arrival takes place on the following day | Arrival takes place one hour later | The fare is increased by one unit | One additional stop is made
exam: Whereas a class commencing at 4:30 continues for forty-five minutes, state the time of its conclusion. | 5:15 | 5:45 | 5:30 | 5:00
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a "last entry" time. | It may fall before closing time, after which admission is refused | It is invariably identical to closing time | It denotes the time of the first admission | It applies only upon Saturdays
